    "q-47657": {
      "dom_id": "step-q47657",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "Which of the following completes the statement so that it is true:\n\n*If the unique solution of $Ax=0,$ where $A$ is a $2\\times 2$-matrix, is $⟨0,0⟩$, then $\\text{__________}$ .*\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0$\n- $A$ is invertible\n- ${A}^{-1}$ does not exist",
      "worked_solution": "Recall that given $A=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.  \n\nNow, since the third part of the invertible matrix theorem is true, all the other parts of the theorem are true too. Therefore, according to the theorem, only options I and II give us true statements:\n\n- *If the unique solution of $Ax=0$ is $⟨0,0⟩$, then $det(A)\\ne 0$ .* $\\,✓$\n\n- *If the unique solution of $Ax=0$ is $⟨0,0⟩$, then $A$ is invertible .*$\\,✓$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
