    "q-128146": {
      "dom_id": "step-q128146",
      "name": "Question 6",
      "problem": "Consider the lines $x+y=3$ and $x-ky=1$, where $k$ is a constant. Complete the following statement so that it is true:\n\n*If the lines intersect at exactly one point, then $\\text{__________}\\,.$*",
      "worked_solution": "Recall that given $A=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{11} & {a}_{12} \\\\ {a}_{21} & {a}_{22}\\end{aligned}]$, then according to the invertible matrix theorem the following statements are equivalent:    \n\n- $A$ is invertible.\n\n- The system $Ax=b$ has exactly one solution for any $2\\times 1$ column vector $b.$\n\n- The system $Ax=0$ has the unique zero solution.\n\n- The lines ${a}_{11}x+{a}_{12}y={b}_{1}$ and ${a}_{21}x+{a}_{22}y={b}_{2}$ intersect at exactly one point.\n\n- $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now check our statements.\n\nAccording to the invertible matrix theorem, if the lines $x+y=3$ and $x-ky=1$  intersect at exactly one point, then \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -k\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nis invertible and $det(A)\\ne 0.$ \nAdditionally, the unique solution to $Ax=0$ is $⟨0,0⟩$ and\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -k\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nhas exactly one solution, which is the intersection point of the lines.\n\nFinally, notice that if $k$ can't be equal to $-1,$ since  $det[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -(-1)\\end{aligned}]=0.$\n\nTherefore, the true statement is as follows:\n\n*f the lines intersect at exactly one point, then $det[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -k\\end{aligned}]\\ne 0.$*",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
